STYLE: A magnificent hand-made toy theatre of printed cardboard, each piece a flat cutout of thick 4 mm card with visible raw brown cut edges. Painted in Chinese charcoal, soft graphite and luminous dry chalk pastel on textured blue-grey cotton rag paper, with coal-dust smudges and broad gentle strokes. Tender, recognizably Chinese faces, restrained human expressions, round brass split pins on articulated puppets. A fictional Chinese-speaking coal-mining town over several decades: worn blue cotton work jackets, simple homes, a pithead wheel, timbered mine entrance, a telescope, and later a planetarium. Palette: coal black #252B30, graphite #4E5962, paper slate #A3ADB4, chalk cream #ECE8DB, faded workwear blue #526C7B, warm ochre #B99159, lamp amber #DFB87C, deep star indigo #24334F, tiny chalk-silver stars #DCE6EA. Contrast the small amber domestic light with an immense indigo star canopy, ending in one warm light on two hands. Beautiful printed-card craft, quiet dignity, familial love and remembered labour.
CONSTRAINTS: No text, writing, lettering, numbers, labels, logos, signatures or watermarks. No national or company emblems. No photographic people, sculpted 3D characters, plastic, glossy finish or anime. No gore or graphic injury. Everything is flat painted card; actual rods, strings, sky, dust and light come from the theatre. A blind father remains alive, dignified, curious and loved. His sight is never magically restored.
Use case: illustration-story. Create wide key art for a physical toy-theatre production. In the foreground LEFT sits a Chinese father of about seventy, grey hair, a lined kind face, blue work collar and worn brown-grey coat, dark trousers, upright on a simple wooden padded chair. He is blind but alive, smiling gently and listening; his open right-facing palm rests towards his daughter. His plain white cane leans beside the chair. To his RIGHT is his grown daughter, early thirties, dark hair in a low bun, cream blouse, long petrol-blue cardigan, a thin rust-red scarf and dark skirt. She leans towards him, facing LEFT, tracing a constellation into his open palm. The two people and their touching hands are the emotional centre. Their fingers are human and clear, not fused. Above and behind them the stage opens into a magnificent indigo planetarium canopy with tiny silver chalk stars and thin dome ribs. On the far LEFT, in a colder memory layer, are an industrial coal pithead wheel, simple workers' houses and a tiny amber kitchen where the same father as a black-haired miner traces stars into his seven-year-old daughter's hand. The little girl has a bob haircut and rust scarf. No duplicate main figures in the foreground. The past and present are layers of real cut-card scenery. One small brass planetarium projector below the canopy. A simple dark proscenium frames the composition. Everything has hand-cut card edges and discreet rods and strings. The sky is a lit cyclorama and stars are theatre pinlights. Landscape 1536 by 1024 or larger. No text.
